---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import FaqList from '../components/FaqList.astro';
import { link, upper } from '../lib/site';
import { getGraph, getStats, getPoopNeighbours, difficultyOf } from '../lib/graph';
import { releasedPuzzles } from '../lib/archive';
import { breadcrumbLd, faqLd } from '../lib/seo';

const { words } = getGraph();
const stats = getStats();
const ring = getPoopNeighbours();
const published = releasedPuzzles();

const playable = [...words.values()].filter((w) => w.distance >= 1);
const bands = (['Easy', 'Medium', 'Hard'] as const).map((key) => ({
  key,
  count: playable.filter((w) => difficultyOf(w.distance) === key).length,
}));

// Distance histogram, longest first for the visual, plus the busiest bucket for the bars.
const rows = Object.entries(stats.distance_histogram)
  .map(([d, count]) => ({ distance: Number(d), count }))
  .sort((a, b) => a.distance - b.distance);
const peak = Math.max(...rows.map((r) => r.count));
const hottest = rows.reduce((a, b) => (b.count > a.count ? b : a));
const hardest = stats.farthest_words.filter(([, d]) => d === stats.max_distance);

const title = 'Word Ladder Statistics — Step Counts, Hardest Words and the Target Ring';
const description = `How the four-letter ladder graph actually looks: all ${stats.word_count.toLocaleString('en-US')} playable words, the full step-count distribution, the ${stats.max_distance}-step record holders and the ${ring.length} words that finish in a single move.`;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How many four-letter words are in the ladder?',
    a: `There are ${stats.word_count.toLocaleString('en-US')} playable words. Every one of them is connected to POOP, so no word is a dead end, and each has a fixed shortest distance measured in single-letter moves.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the hardest word?',
    a: `The longest ladders take ${stats.max_distance} steps, and only ${hardest.length} words sit that far out: ${hardest.map(([w]) => upper(w)).join(', ')}. Anything beyond a few steps from the target is a genuine hunt rather than a warm-up.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'How many words solve it in one move?',
    a: `Exactly ${ring.length} words reach POOP in a single step: ${ring.map(upper).join(', ')}. They form the target ring, and every long ladder is ultimately a route to one of them.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Why do most words sit around five steps?',
    a: `Because the graph is a lopsided bell centred on the middle. Each move can only change the distance by one, so the number of words available grows for the first few rings and then falls away; the ${hottest.distance}-step bucket is the widest with ${hottest.count.toLocaleString('en-US')} words. That is also why the daily puzzle so often lands in the Medium band.`,
  },
];
const jsonLd = [
  breadcrumbLd([{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Statistics', path: '/statistics/' }]),
  faqLd(faqs),
];
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/statistics/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Statistics' }]} />

  <h1>Word ladder statistics</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The whole ladder graph, measured. Every number below is computed from the same graph the answers
    and the solver use, so this is not an estimate — it is a census of all
    {stats.word_count.toLocaleString('en-US')} playable words and how far each one sits from POOP.
  </p>

  <div class="grid three">
    <section class="card tight">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Playable words</h3>
      <p style="margin:0"><strong style="font-size:1.6rem">{stats.word_count.toLocaleString('en-US')}</strong></p>
      <p class="muted" style="margin:4px 0 0">All connected to POOP — no dead ends.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card tight">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Longest ladder</h3>
      <p style="margin:0"><strong style="font-size:1.6rem">{stats.max_distance} steps</strong></p>
      <p class="muted" style="margin:4px 0 0">Reached by {hardest.map(([w]) => upper(w)).join(', ')}.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card tight">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">One-move finishes</h3>
      <p style="margin:0"><strong style="font-size:1.6rem">{ring.length} words</strong></p>
      <p class="muted" style="margin:4px 0 0">The ring that every hard ladder hunts for.</p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <h2>Step-count distribution</h2>
  <p>
    Distance is the minimum number of single-letter moves needed to reach POOP. It behaves like
    distance on a map: a move always changes it by exactly one, so a word can never jump from four
    steps out to two. That is why the counts below form a smooth, lopsided bell rather than a scatter
    — and why the middle buckets dominate.
  </p>
  <ul class="hist">
    {rows.map((r) => (
      <li>
        <span class="lab">{r.distance} {r.distance === 1 ? 'step' : 'steps'}</span>
        <span class="bar"><i style={`width:${Math.max(1.5, (r.count / peak) * 100).toFixed(1)}%`}></i></span>
        <span class="n">{r.count.toLocaleString('en-US')}</span>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">
    Read the shape from the bottom up: POOP itself is the single 0-step entry, the count climbs to a
    peak at {hottest.distance} steps ({hottest.count.toLocaleString('en-US')} words), then tails off
    sharply. Only {rows.filter((r) => r.distance >= 9).reduce((s, r) => s + r.count, 0)} words sit
    9 or more steps away, which is why the longest puzzles feel rare.
  </p>

  <h2>The {stats.max_distance}-step record holders</h2>
  <p>
    Only {hardest.length} words take the full {stats.max_distance} steps:
    {hardest.map(([w], i) => (
      <>
        {i > 0 && ', '}
        <a href={link(`/from/${w}/`)}>{upper(w)}</a>
      </>
    ))}
    . They are not longer because the words are unusual — they are longer because their letters barely
    overlap with anything useful near the target, so the route has to detour through several
    intermediate shapes before it can join the ring.
  </p>
  <p>
    Just behind them sit the 10-step words, including
    {stats.farthest_words.filter(([, d]) => d === 10).slice(0, 5).map(([w], i) => (
      <>
        {i > 0 && ', '}
        <a href={link(`/from/${w}/`)}>{upper(w)}</a>
      </>
    ))}
    . The gap between 10 and 11 steps is where the ladder graph runs out of room: past that point
    there simply is no word far enough from POOP to be further away.
  </p>

  <h2>The target ring: words one move from POOP</h2>
  <p>
    A single step from the goal are {ring.length} words. They matter out of all proportion to their
    number, because every optimal ladder ends by arriving at one of them:
  </p>
  <ul class="linklist">
    {ring.map((w) => (
      <li><a href={link(`/from/${w}/`)}>{upper(w)} — 1 step</a></li>
    ))}
  </ul>
  <p>
    Notice how they are built. POOP is <span class="mono">P-O-O-P</span>: four positions, three
    distinct letters, one repeated. A word joins the ring by keeping three of those four letters in
    place and swapping the fourth, which gives the boilerplate <em>-OOP</em> family (BOOP, COOP, GOOP,
    HOOP, LOOP) and the <em>POO-</em> family (POOD-style tail changes like POOF, POOL, POOR).
    Because the goal is so structured, the ring is small — and that is the single most useful thing
    to memorise about this game.
  </p>

  <h2>Difficulty bands</h2>
  <p>
    The daily puzzle labels itself by shortest length, and the same bands apply here. The split below
    covers all {playable.length.toLocaleString('en-US')} words that are not POOP itself.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Band</th><th>Shortest ladder</th><th>Words</th><th>Share</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {bands.map((b) => (
        <tr>
          <td class={`diff ${b.key}`}>{b.key}</td>
          <td>{b.key === 'Easy' ? '1–3 steps' : b.key === 'Medium' ? '4–5 steps' : '6+ steps'}</td>
          <td>{b.count.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
          <td>{((b.count / playable.length) * 100).toFixed(1)}%</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Medium is comfortably the largest band, which is not a coincidence: it contains the peak buckets at
    4 and 5 steps. That is the reason a typical daily puzzle feels solvable but not trivial — the
    graph is simply densest there. Hard words are rarer in the pool but over-represented in how long
    they take to read, which is exactly why the solver is more useful on them than on an Easy
    warm-up.
  </p>

  <h2>The published archive</h2>
  <p>
    {published.length.toLocaleString('en-US')} puzzles have gone live so far, one per day, each with a
    fixed starting word and a fixed minimum length. Because the graph never changes, the step count of
    a past puzzle is permanent — it can be recomputed at any time and will come out the same. You can
    browse the lot in the <a href={link('/archive/')}>answer archive</a>, or jump straight to a single
    word with the <a href={link('/solver/')}>solver</a>.
  </p>

  <FaqList items={faqs} />

  <section class="card">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Keep exploring</h2>
    <ul class="linklist">
      <li><a href={link('/from/')}>Word index by difficulty</a></li>
      <li><a href={link('/data-methodology/')}>How these numbers are computed</a></li>
      <li><a href={link('/how-to-play/')}>How to play</a></li>
      <li><a href={link('/archive/')}>Answer archive</a></li>
      <li><a href={link('/solver/')}>Solver</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>
